1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,500
On Earth, when a building catches fire,
you run outside.

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:08,800
Three hundred miles above Earth, you are locked
in an airtight pressure vessel with your fire.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:11,200
There is no outside.

4
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:15,500
And microgravity fire doesn't rise to the ceiling
where smoke detectors are mounted.

5
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:18,500
Buoyancy vanishes.

6
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:23,200
Flames form silent, suffocating blue spheres
that creep backward inside ventilation ducts—

7
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:27,500
fed by the crew's own life-support fans.

8
00:00:27,800 --> 00:00:32,200
To protect future lunar and Martian crews,
NASA conducted 145 real flight burns.

9
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:36,500
We are Team FLARE-X from Bangladesh:
Naim, Mahim, Masum, Muntaha, Hamza, and Nahid.

10
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:39,000
And this is FLARE-X.

11
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:44,000
When Artemis engineers evaluate a new cabin material
at eighteen percent oxygen, where do they look?

12
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:48,800
NASA's combustion data is scattered across
300-page reports, spreadsheets, and unindexed videos.

13
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:53,800
Worse, generic AI chatbots hallucinate numbers
because they don't understand combustion physics.

14
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:58,500
Droplet burning in FLEX does not behave like
solid polymer flame-spread in BASS-Two.

15
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:03,500
Different fuels, different chamber pressures,
and different geometries.

16
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,800
Researchers don't need a chatbot that guesses.

17
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:13,200
They need a system that finds physically comparable
experiments, checks boundaries, and proves provenance.

18
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:17,800
FLARE-X bridges that gap
with a six-step closed-loop pipeline.

19
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:23,000
When a scenario is entered, our system routes it
to its verified combustion family,

20
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:27,500
matches conditions using multi-dimensional Gower distance,
and retrieves the closest empirical flight burns.

21
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,000
For supported solid fuels, our family-specialized
gradient-boosted model predicts extinction regimes

22
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:38,500
with an honest 79.3% grouped cross-validation accuracy—
a 27% lift over baseline.

23
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,500
But FLARE-X has an essential safety rule:
an Experimental Envelope Guard.

24
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,800
Before returning any prediction, it verifies whether
the requested oxygen, airflow, and pressure

25
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,800
are backed by actual NASA flight runs.

26
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:57,200
Here is our live project in action.

27
00:01:57,500 --> 00:02:02,200
In the Experimental Support Map, every dot
is a real NASA spaceflight burn.

28
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:06,000
The star is our exploration scenario.

29
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:11,500
Watch what happens when we sweep oxygen down
from twenty-one percent to eighteen percent.

30
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,500
The system updates live.

31
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:20,500
The evidence re-ranks, showing nearby BASS-Two tests,
and predicts flame quenching at forty-two seconds.

32
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:25,500
Now, let's push oxygen down to thirteen percent.

33
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:29,200
Look at the interface.

34
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:32,800
FLARE-X did not guess.

35
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:36,500
It triggered an Envelope Refusal.

36
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:42,200
It withholds the prediction because NASA never tested
these flow conditions at thirteen percent oxygen.

37
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Every output links to primary NASA NTRS papers
and PSI datasets.

38
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:53,500
Because in deep space,
the absence of evidence is not evidence of safety.

39
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,000
FLARE-X:
Evidence-bounded AI for microgravity fire science.
